# Issue #1021, the other direction: the one-token lookahead that makes `(pi == pi)` a parenthesized
# expression must NOT swallow a genuine `parenthesized-call` (grammar.md:215). Each line here is a
# trap for a naive "an operator follows, so this is an expression" rule, and each fails differently
# if the rule is wrong:
#   - `and`/`or` are the variadic logic HEADS here, not infix uses, so the lookahead must look PAST
#     the head token before judging;
#   - `(pi)` is a zero-argument call with nothing following at all — `)` is no operator;
#   - an ordinary fixed call, a word-building call, and the variadic `(print …)` form must be
#     completely untouched.
print (and true false)
print (or true false)
print (pi)
print (round 1)
print (word "a" "b")
(print 1 2 3)
